Idea in plain Yorkshire:
- Keep dist[i] = minimum edges frae 1 t'i, start with INF for owt but dist[1]=0.
- Keep adjacency lists for t'graph.
- On query type 1: if dist[x] is INF print -1 else print dist[x].
- On query type 2 (add u->v): push v onto g[u]. If dist[u] is known (nowt INF) an' dist[v] > dist[u]+1 then set dist[v] = dist[u]+1 and run a BFS-like propagation from v: whenever a node's distance drops use its outgoing edges t'relax neighbours. Distances only ever go down, so this stays correct.

This approach is efficient enough wi' N up t'1000 an' M up t'500k because we only relax when distances actually improve.

